              question:
                'Explain the cost of quality. Classify prevention, appraisal, internal failure and external failure costs and give two examples of each in a software project.',
              explain:
                '**What is being asked.** Cost of quality (COQ) is the total money a project spends because quality is not perfect — both the money spent to get quality right and the money lost when it goes wrong. The examiner wants the four categories, not a vague list of expenses.\n\n**The four categories.** Prevention cost — money spent so defects never appear (training, process definition, tools, reviews planned in advance). Appraisal cost — money spent to find defects that already exist (testing, inspection, measurement). Internal failure cost — money lost when a defect is found before release (rework, re-testing, scrap of the build). External failure cost — money lost when a defect reaches the customer (support, hotfix, refund, penalty, lost goodwill).\n\n**The idea that carries marks.** Prevention and appraisal are the cost of getting quality; failure costs are the cost of not getting it. Failure costs grow by an order of magnitude as a defect moves from code to customer.\n\n**How to score the 5 marks.** Definition (1) · four categories with two software examples each (3) · the prevention-versus-failure conclusion (1).',
              answer:
                '**Cost of quality (COQ)** is the total cost of achieving quality plus the total cost of not achieving it. It converts quality from an opinion into a number that management can act on.\n\n| Category | Meaning | Software examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Prevention cost** | spent so that defects are never introduced | quality training, process definition, code review standards, static analysis licence, requirement workshops |\n| **Appraisal cost** | spent to detect defects that already exist | unit, integration and system testing, peer reviews and inspections, test environment and test data, defect tracking tool |\n| **Internal failure cost** | defect found before release | rework of code and design, re-testing after a fix, scrapped build, idle team time while a fix is made |\n| **External failure cost** | defect found after release | customer support and escalation, hotfix and emergency release, refund or service credit, penalty for SLA breach, lost reputation and lost repeat business |\n\n**Formula:** COQ = prevention + appraisal + internal failure + external failure.\n\n**How the mix behaves.** A team that spends nothing on prevention pays heavily in failure. As prevention and appraisal rise, internal and external failure fall faster than the rise, so the total COQ falls. The usual maturity goal is prevention and appraisal to be the larger part of the total and external failure to be the smallest part.\n\n**Cost of poor quality (COPQ)** = internal failure + external failure. This is the number a manager tracks month after month, because it is the money that was lost rather than the money that was invested.\n\n**Exam line:** prevention and appraisal are investments, failure costs are losses; a mature project shifts money from failure to prevention.',
              diagram: 'coq-overview',
