              question:
                'Explain the Taguchi quality loss function. Describe the smaller-the-better, larger-the-better and nominal-the-best cases and explain why loss occurs even inside the specification limits.',
              explain:
                '**What is being asked.** Taguchi changed the way quality is judged: from pass or fail against a tolerance band to a continuous loss that grows as the value moves away from the target. The examiner wants the loss function, its three forms and the tolerance-versus-target argument.\n\n**The loss function.** L = k x (y minus m) squared, where y is the measured value, m is the target and k is a cost constant. The curve is a parabola: zero loss exactly at target, rising on both sides. Loss is measured in money, not in defect counts.\n\n**The three cases.** Smaller-the-better — the target is zero and any increase is loss (defects, noise, response time). Larger-the-better — the target is the maximum possible and any reduction is loss (strength, battery life, coverage). Nominal-the-best — a specific value is the target and deviation in either direction is loss (a shaft diameter, a password length, a temperature).\n\n**Why inside tolerance is still loss.** Traditional inspection says a value inside the specification is acceptable and the loss appears only outside. Taguchi says the customer notices small deviations every day: a value at the edge of tolerance behaves differently from a value at target, so loss has already started before the limit is crossed. The limit is where the loss becomes unacceptable, not where it begins.\n\n**How to score the 5 marks.** Formula and curve (2) · three cases with examples (2) · the tolerance-versus-target argument (1).',
              answer:
                '**Taguchi quality loss function** states that any deviation of a product characteristic from its target value causes a loss to society, and that the loss grows as the square of the deviation.\n\n**Formula**\nL = k x (y − m)²\nwhere L is the loss in money, y the measured value, m the target value and k a constant that converts squared deviation into currency.\n\nThe curve is a parabola: the loss is zero only at the target and rises on both sides.\n\n**The three forms**\n| Form | Target | Loss grows when | Software example |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Smaller-the-better** | zero | the value increases | defect count, response time, memory use, crash rate |\n| **Larger-the-better** | maximum possible | the value decreases | test coverage, throughput, battery life, uptime |\n| **Nominal-the-best** | a stated value | the value moves either way | field length, timeout value, batch size, pump pressure |\n\n**Why loss occurs inside the specification limits**\nTraditional inspection divides values into acceptable and rejectable at the specification limit, and treats every acceptable value as equally good. Taguchi rejects this. A component that sits at the edge of tolerance is already behaving differently from the same component at target — it wears faster, it interacts worse with its neighbours, and the customer feels it long before the limit is crossed. The specification limit is the point where the loss becomes unacceptable, **not** the point where the loss begins.\n\nThe practical consequence is well known: two suppliers can both ship 100 percent within tolerance while one has a tight spread around target and the other hugs the limits. Traditional inspection cannot separate them; the loss function gives the wider-spread supplier a higher loss figure, so quality is measured by distance from target rather than by distance from the limit.\n\n**Exam line:** judge quality by the distance from target, not by the distance from the limit — a value inside tolerance can already be losing money.',
              diagram: 'taguchi-loss-overview',
