              question:
                'Differentiate between product quality and process quality with suitable examples. Why is process quality treated as the better predictor of long-term quality?',
              explain:
                '**What is being asked.** Two words that sound alike but answer different questions: product quality asks whether the delivered software satisfies the user, process quality asks whether the way the software was produced is capable of producing quality repeatedly.\n\n**Product quality attributes.** Functionality, reliability, usability, efficiency, maintainability, portability, security — the attributes in a quality model such as ISO 9126 or ISO 25010. These are measured on the delivered artefact.\n\n**Process quality attributes.** Requirement clarity, design review coverage, coding standard compliance, review and inspection rate, test coverage, defect density by phase, defect removal effectiveness, schedule adherence. These are measured on the way work is done.\n\n**The causal argument.** A good process produces a good product predictably; a good product produced by a weak process is luck and does not repeat on the next release. Process metrics also arrive early, when a correction is still cheap, while product metrics arrive only when the build exists.\n\n**How to score the 5 marks.** Clear definitions of both (2) · examples or a short table (2) · the predictability argument (1).',
              answer:
                '**Product quality** is the degree to which the delivered software satisfies stated and implied needs. It is judged from outside, on the artefact.\n\n**Process quality** is the degree to which the development and testing process is capable of producing quality repeatedly. It is judged from inside, on the way the work is performed.\n\n| Point | Product quality | Process quality |\n|---|---|---|\n| Question answered | does the software satisfy the user? | is our way of working able to produce quality again? |\n| Measured on | the built product | the activities that produced it |\n| Attributes | functionality, reliability, usability, efficiency, maintainability, portability, security | requirement clarity, design and code review coverage, coding standard compliance, test coverage, defect density by phase, defect removal effectiveness |\n| Measured when | after the build exists | throughout the project, from the first phase |\n| Example | the payment module books the correct amount and never double charges | every payment change passes design review and 90 percent branch coverage before merge |\n| Owner | test team, reviewers, users | the whole organisation — management, process group, team |\n\n**Why process quality predicts long-term quality**\n1. **Cause and effect.** The product is the output of the process. If reviews are skipped and tests are designed late, defects will appear; if they are done, defects are removed early.\n2. **Repeatability.** A good product from a weak process is luck, and luck does not repeat on the next release. A capable process produces good products release after release.\n3. **Early signal.** Process metrics are visible while a correction is still cheap. Product metrics are visible only when the build exists, when fixing a defect costs far more.\n4. **Continuous improvement.** Process data (defect density by phase, review rate, DRE) shows where to change the way work is done, so the improvement is permanent instead of being a one-off fix.\n\n**Balanced conclusion for the exam:** manage the process so that the product is predictable, and keep measuring the product so that the process does not become an end in itself. Product quality is the outcome, process quality is the cause.\n\n**Exam line:** product quality is what the customer gets today; process quality is what the customer keeps getting tomorrow.',
