              question:
                'Differentiate between quality assurance and quality control. Give one example of each from a software project and explain why quality control alone cannot deliver quality.',
              explain:
                '**What is being asked.** The classic QA versus QC distinction, applied rather than recited. Definitions, examples, and the argument about why detection alone is insufficient.\n\n**QA** is process oriented and preventive: it builds the system that stops defects from being created — standards, reviews, training, audits, process definition. **QC** is product oriented and detective: it examines the output and rejects what does not meet the standard — testing, inspection, review of work products.\n\n**Examples.** QA — mandatory peer review before merge. QC — executing the login test cases on the build.\n\n**Why QC alone fails.** QC can only find defects that already exist, and it finds them late, when the defect is expensive; it cannot stop the next defect of the same kind because it produces no process change. Without QA the same defect class returns with every release.\n\n**How to score the 5 marks.** Definitions of both with orientation (2) · examples and comparison table (2) · the limitation argument (1).',
              answer:
                '**Quality assurance (QA)** is the set of planned and systematic activities that create confidence that the process will produce quality — standards, process definition, reviews, training, audits. It is **process oriented and preventive**.\n\n**Quality control (QC)** is the set of activities that examine a work product and compare it with the specification to decide acceptance — testing, inspection, product reviews. It is **product oriented and detective**.\n\n| Point | Quality assurance | Quality control |\n|---|---|---|\n| Focus | the process | the product |\n| Nature | preventive | detective |\n| Question | is our way of working able to produce quality? | does this output meet the specification? |\n| Activities | process definition, standards, reviews of process, training, audits, metrics | test execution, inspection, product review, defect reporting |\n| Performed by | the whole team, supported by the quality group | the test and inspection team |\n| Timing | planned from the start of the project | after work products exist |\n| Result | confidence and fewer defects created | defects found and rejected |\n\n**Example from a software project**\n- **QA:** the team agrees that no code is merged without a peer review and a green automated build, and the process group audits compliance every sprint.\n- **QC:** the tester executes the payment test cases on the build and reports that the double-charge case fails.\n\n**Why quality control alone cannot deliver quality**\n1. QC works on output that already exists, so it can only detect, not prevent. Every defect it finds has already cost the work of creating it.\n2. QC acts late in the cycle, when a defect is most expensive to fix.\n3. QC produces no process change. The same defect class returns on the next release because nothing about how the code was written changed.\n4. Some quality attributes cannot be tested in at all — maintainability, review discipline and requirement clarity have to be built in by the process.\n\n**Conclusion:** QA and QC are complementary, not alternatives. QA reduces the number of defects created and QC reduces the number that escape. A project with strong QC and no QA finds many defects forever; a project with strong QA and adequate QC finds few defects and ships them rarely.\n\n**Exam line:** QA prevents, QC detects — detection without prevention keeps paying for the same defect.',
