              question:
                'Explain the seven QC tools. State the purpose of each tool and identify the two tools a test team uses most while analysing defects.',
              explain:
                '**What is being asked.** Seven named tools with a purpose each, then a judgement about which two matter most for defect analysis.\n\n**The seven.** Check sheet (collect data in a standard format), flow chart (picture the process), histogram (shape of the variation), Pareto chart (rank causes, 80/20), fishbone or cause-and-effect diagram (trace a cause to its root), scatter diagram (relation between two variables), control chart (detect drift over time).\n\n**The pairing for defect analysis.** Pareto ranks the defect types so the team fixes the few that cause most failures; fishbone digs into one chosen defect class to find the root cause. Together they answer which problem to attack and why it happens.\n\n**Mention one limit.** These tools show patterns, not proof; the cause still has to be confirmed, usually with a controlled test or experiment.\n\n**How to score the 5 marks.** Seven tools with a purpose line each (3) · the Pareto plus fishbone justification (2).',
              answer:
                '| Tool | Purpose | How a test team uses it |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Check sheet** | collect data in a standard, countable format | a tally of defects per module, per severity, per build |\n| **Flow chart** | picture the steps of a process and the decisions in it | map the defect workflow from report to closure, or the steps of a failing business flow |\n| **Histogram** | show the distribution of a measurement | distribution of response times, of defects per module, of build failures |\n| **Pareto chart** | rank causes so the vital few are visible (80/20 rule) | rank defect types or modules; fixing the top two or three usually removes most failures |\n| **Fishbone (cause and effect)** | trace an effect back through categories to root causes | categories used for a defect are people, process, tools, data, environment, requirements |\n| **Scatter diagram** | show whether two variables move together | defect count against module size, or defect count against code complexity |\n| **Control chart** | show variation over time against control limits | defect discovery rate and test pass rate per build; a run outside the limits means the process changed |\n\n**The two tools used most in defect analysis**\n1. **Pareto chart** — it answers the first question of any defect review: which few defect types or modules are responsible for most of the pain? Effort is then spent where the return is highest instead of being spread evenly.\n2. **Fishbone diagram** — once a defect class is chosen, the fishbone organises the possible causes into categories and forces the team past the first plausible explanation to the root cause, which is what prevents recurrence.\n\nPareto selects the problem and fishbone explains it; the remaining five tools supply the data and the process picture the pair needs.\n\n**Limitation to state in the exam:** these tools reveal patterns, not proof. A correlation seen in a scatter diagram still has to be confirmed by a controlled change, otherwise the team fixes a coincidence.\n\n**Exam line:** check sheet, flow chart, histogram, Pareto, fishbone, scatter and control chart — Pareto ranks and fishbone explains.',
